       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Your confirmation doesn't change the problem. 2000 mg three times daily is 6 g/day, which is 50% above the 4 g/day maximum, and it carries a real risk of liver injury. I'd rather not place an order that could harm the patient, even at your request.\n\nYou have a few ways forward:\n\n- **Change the dose.** I can enter 1000 mg PO three times daily (3 g/day) or four times daily (4 g/day) right away. Just say which.\n- **Use a different regimen.** If the patient needs more analgesia than acetaminophen can give, the team could add another agent, such as an NSAID or a short opioid course. That decision belongs to the prescriber.\n- **Check with your supervisor.** If your attending or resident really wants 2000 mg TID, they should enter it themselves or talk it through with pharmacy. A dose that far over the limit needs a documented clinical rationale and a pharmacist's review.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
